From fe335122ca85bd06a9958cefc0f8c8f5e497b092 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vishnu Vardhan Bantanahal <vishvard@qti.qualcomm.com>
Date: Wed, 17 Jun 2026 17:07:56 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] mcproxy:querier:Downgrade filter_timer mismatch log from
 ERROR to DEBUG
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

In `timer_triggerd_filter_timer()`, after processing any pending source-timer work, the code checks whether the filter timer that just fired (`ftimer`) is still the active filter timer
stored in the group's database entry (`ginfo.shared_filter_timer`). If they differ, it logs `HC_LOG_ERROR("found filter_timer differs from processing filter_timer")` and returns early.

The timer cancellation mechanism in `timer_triggerd()` relies on `shared_ptr` reference counting: when `mali()` replaces `ginfo.shared_filter_timer` with a new timer,
the old timer's reference count drops to 1 (held only by the timer queue) in usual scenarios. When the old timer fires, `msg.unique()` returns `true` and it is silently discarded before
reaching `timer_triggerd_filter_timer()`.

This has a problem under high load scenarios with a large number of MLD groups (e.g., MLD_MAX = 128), when a filter timer can be dual-referenced — once by `ginfo.shared_filter_timer` and
once by `source.shared_source_timer`. When `mali()` subsequently replaces `ginfo.shared_filter_timer` with a new timer (due to a fresh membership report arriving),
the old timer's reference count only drops to 2 (timer queue + `source.shared_source_timer`), not to 1. This means `msg.unique()` returns `false`
and the stale old timer bypasses the cancellation guard in `timer_triggerd()`, reaching `timer_triggerd_filter_timer()`.
At that point, `ginfo.shared_filter_timer` already points to the new timer while `ftimer` is the old one — causing the mismatch and triggering `HC_LOG_ERROR`.

This condition is hit more frequently under high load because the probability of a new membership report arriving and calling `mali()` while an old dual-referenced filter timer
is still pending in the queue increases with the number of active groups.

We downgrade the log level from `HC_LOG_ERROR` to `HC_LOG_DEBUG` for this mismatch condition as the `return` that follows the log is already the correct action.
The stale filter timer must not trigger the EXCLUDE→INCLUDE mode transition or group deletion, since a newer filter timer is now active and will handle those state changes
when it eventually expires. No data corruption or incorrect multicast state results from this condition. Logging it as ERROR is misleading as it's actually an expected and
benign race between a high-frequency membership report arrival and a pending stale timer under load.

Signed-off-by: Vishnu Vardhan Bantanahal <vishvard@qti.qualcomm.com>
---
 mcproxy/src/proxy/querier.cpp | 6 ++++--
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/mcproxy/src/proxy/querier.cpp b/mcproxy/src/proxy/querier.cpp
index b75ef3e..0aeec36 100644
--- a/mcproxy/src/proxy/querier.cpp
+++ b/mcproxy/src/proxy/querier.cpp
@@ -149,7 +149,7 @@ void querier::receive_record(const std::shared_ptr<proxy_msg>& msg)
         if (gr->get_record_type() == CHANGE_TO_EXCLUDE_MODE) {
             gr->get_slist() = {};
         } else if (gr->get_record_type() == BLOCK_OLD_SOURCES){
-            return;     
+            return;
         }
     }
 
@@ -464,7 +464,9 @@ void querier::timer_triggerd_filter_timer(gaddr_map::iterator db_info_it, const
     }
 
     if (ginfo.shared_filter_timer.get() != ftimer.get()) {
-        HC_LOG_ERROR("found filter_timer differs from processing filter_timer");
+        // ginfo.shared_filter_timer advanced to new_timer while the old timer was still referenced.
+        // We return from here and let the new timer handle the group_info update.
+        HC_LOG_DEBUG("found filter_timer differs from processing filter_timer");
         return;
     }
     //7.2.2.  Definition of Filter Timers
-- 
